 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ for matrix construction and inversion [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for the uniform buffer (36–40 bytes) [G].
-- **Performance:** Computation is extremely fast and typically performed on the CPU once per frame or when camera parameters change [G].
-- **Strengths:** Explicit control over FOV and aspect ratio; flexible coordinate system handling [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires manual management of uniform buffers and matrix updates [G].
-- **When to use:** When implementing custom renderers, Gaussian Splatting, or when fine-grained control over camera matrices is needed beyond fixed-function pipeline defaults [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ for matrix construction, as it involves a fixed number of arithmetic operations and matrix multiplications/inversions.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for the matrices themselves (4x4 floats each). The uniform buffer size is fixed (34-40 floats) [S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Matrix construction is extremely fast and typically done once per frame or when camera parameters change. The cost is negligible compared to ray generation and splat rendering.
+- **Strengths:** Explicit control over camera parameters and focal lengths allows for precise tuning of the splat rendering pipeline. The separation of view and projection matrices allows for easy coordinate system conversions.
+- **Limitations:** The method assumes a standard perspective projection. For specialized projections (e.g., orthographic, cube map), additional logic is required. The coordinate system flips mentioned in [S1, unofficial] may not be necessary for all backends, requiring careful API selection.
+- **When to use:** Use this method when setting up the camera for Gaussian Splatting or other volume rendering techniques that require explicit focal length information for shader-based splatting. It is suitable for both training and inference phases where camera parameters may change dynamically.
 
 ## Background
-This method is fundamental to computer graphics, originating from standard perspective projection and look-at camera models [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. The specific variations cited here are adapted for modern real-time Gaussian Splatting implementations using Vulkan [S1, unofficial], WGPU [S2, unofficial], and OpenGL [S3, unofficial]. The explicit computation of tangent FOV and focal lengths is particularly relevant for splat-based rendering where shader uniforms require these values for correct projection [S3, unofficial].
+This method is derived from implementations in Gaussian Splatting libraries, specifically those targeting Vulkan and WGPU backends [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. The explicit calculation of focal lengths and the handling of coordinate system conversions are adaptations for the specific requirements of splat shaders, which often operate in a different coordinate space than standard rasterization pipelines [S3, unofficial]. The use of `glm::lookAt` and `glm::perspective` is common in OpenGL-based implementations [S3, unofficial], while WGPU implementations may require additional matrix conversions [S2, unofficial].
 
